  @{
    Slug = 'custom-wedding-favor-stickers-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Wedding Favor Stickers: Names, Dates and Packing'
    Description = 'Plan custom wedding favor stickers around names, dates, guest counts, jars, bags and boxes, botanical artwork, proof approval and packing deadlines.'
    Category = 'Event and favor stickers'
    CardDescription = 'Control names, dates, counts and original botanical graphics across favor jars, bags and boxes before the packing day.'
    Alt = 'Wedding favor jars boxes and paper bags with fully printed original floral and monogram stickers beside a proof checklist and label sheets'
    Caption = 'Keep the design consistent while checking names, dates, counts and the actual favor packaging.'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can one wedding favor label be used on jars, bags and boxes?', 'A shared artwork style can work, but each package needs its own size and surface check. Test labels on the final jar, bag and box before approving one construction for all three.'),
      @('What should be approved on a personalized wedding sticker proof?', 'Approve spelling, names, date format, artwork version, final dimensions, cutline and quantity split. For guest-specific designs, compare every version with the final source list.'),
      @('How should I count a wedding favor sticker order?', 'Count labels by placement and package type, then add an explicitly agreed spare quantity. Total guest count alone does not state how many labels each favor needs.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>Wedding favor stickers look like a small purchase until someone is packing jars the night before the event. The names have to be right. The date has to be right. The round label has to fit the actual lid. I would settle those three details before talking about gold foil or a custom contour.</p>
  <p>The person ordering may be a couple, a planner or a gift supplier. Each sees a different piece of the project. The couple notices the design; the planner watches the schedule; the supplier needs files and counts that will survive packing. A useful specification gives all three a common reference.</p>
  <h2>Use the Final Favor Package for Size Decisions</h2>
  <p>A jar lid, a folded paper bag and a small box can share an artwork family without sharing one sticker size. Measure the usable area in mm, avoiding raised lid rims, folds and box opening lines unless the label is deliberately intended to cross them.</p>
  <p>Consider an event using mini jars for one group of guests and paper bags for another. A label sized for the jar might look lost on the bag. I would retain the floral motif and names, then make a separate layout for each placement. This is a packaging decision, not a reason to redesign the whole wedding identity.</p>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Package</th><th>Measurement</th><th>Proof or trial focus</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Jar lid</td><td>Usable diameter in mm</td><td>Rim clearance and centered artwork</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Jar side</td><td>Width and height in mm</td><td>Curve, label seam and small text</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Paper bag</td><td>Front panel or folded closure</td><td>Placement and opening</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Gift box</td><td>Visible panel and lid seam</td><td>Presentation and box finish</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Guest-specific item</td><td>Final-size name area</td><td>Every personalized version</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <h2>Personalization Needs a Source List</h2>
  <p>Repeated names and one date are relatively simple. Individual guest names change the approval job. Keep one source list with the final spelling, version ID and required quantity. Do not combine an old spreadsheet, a message correction and a screenshot and expect the printer to guess which is final.</p>
  <p>I would ask one named person to approve the completed set of versions. An attractive sample proof for the first name is not approval for all the others. If the date format is numeric, confirm the intended reading explicitly. A familiar date arrangement in one country can be interpreted differently elsewhere.</p>
  <h2>Give the Artwork Character Without Crowding the Names</h2>
  <p>Original flowers, leaves, fruit motifs, venue-inspired line drawings and monograms can make favor labels feel personal. There is no need to turn them into empty white circles to avoid copying a designer's artwork. Use an owned or authorized design and make the names and occasion readable.</p>
  <p>Script lettering is worth testing at final size. A thin stroke that looks delicate on a large screen may look weak on a small lid. If a decorative motif takes up too much space, I would simplify it before shrinking the names. The favor should be recognizable while someone holds it, not only in a close-up photograph.</p>
  <h2>Count Placements, Then Count Spares</h2>
  <p>As a planning example, 120 favors using a side label and a lid label require 240 applied labels before spares. That is arithmetic for that example, not a minimum order policy. Add the agreed spare quantity explicitly and split the total by label size and artwork version.</p>
  <p>Guest counts may change. Packaging can also be replaced late. Record the final container reference and approval date. If the buyer expects a second order, ask which elements can remain stable so a small count adjustment does not require rebuilding every file.</p>
  <h2>Let Packing Speed Influence the Supply Format</h2>
  <p>Sheets keep small quantities and multiple layouts organized. Rolls can suit repeated application when orientation and peel access are clear. Individual pieces may be useful for certain sets but require more counting and sorting. The best format is the one the packing team can handle calmly.</p>
  <p>A digital proof can check text, placement and cutline. It cannot establish the actual adhesive behavior on the jar, tissue or box. If the event depends on a particular finish or opening result, include time for the appropriate physical check before bulk approval. Confirm the production and shipping schedule with the supplier rather than inferring it from an attractive image.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Identify the final jar, bag or box</li><li>Measure every label placement</li><li>Approve names and date format from one source</li><li>Review the design at final printed size</li><li>Separate quantity by placement and version</li><li>Record the agreed spare allowance</li><li>Test peeling, application and opening</li><li>Confirm packing and arrival requirements</li></ul>
  <p>Use our <a href="/products/custom-gift-stickers/">gift sticker category</a> and <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a> to prepare the request. The <a href="/blog/custom-sticker-samples-and-digital-proofs/">proof and sample guide</a> helps choose the next review. I would finish with a packed favor on the table and the approved name list beside it. That is a much calmer place to find a mistake than the night before the wedding.</p>
